Cut native views' holes in the wgpu renderer, so every wgpu target (Linux, Android, web) can show one.

What: `PrimitiveBatch::NativeViews` draws, in paint order, each view's bounds clipped to its content mask as
transparent black with blending off, as the Metal renderer's `native_view_fragment` does (Chromium's
`ReplaceExistingQuadWithHolePunch` semantics): what was painted beneath is erased, what is painted after covers it.
Holes are passed as `Quad`s, so both instance transports (storage buffers, WebGL2's texture) decode them with the
existing `load_quad`; the pipeline tests depth like other non-quad batches and writes none, so opaque quads painted
after a view (drawn early, front to back) stay over its hole. While the window has native views attached (the new
`Scene::native_views_attached`, set by the window each frame, shown or not) the surface uses its transparent alpha
mode (premultiplied where offered; `wanted_alpha_mode`, the one rule), whatever the window asked for, and the hole
pipeline exists; once the last view is dropped both go. So showing and hiding a view (a tab switch) rebuilds nothing,
and a window that never attaches a view never builds the hole pipeline nor writes its bind group (built lazily,
`update_pipelines` compares the mode and the views with what the pipelines were built for). The mode is logged when
the first view is attached. `WgpuRenderer::native_view_support()` says what blends the window (`NativeViewSupport`: the
surface's mode, or a WebGL2 canvas's premultiplied context whatever mode wgpu reports, which the log names) or fails
with the typed `NativeViewsUnsupported` on a surface that offers no blending mode (NVIDIA's Vulkan WSI being the
known risk), so a platform can fail `NativeView::new` and the app fall back.
`set_native_view_hole_inset` (kept only on the renderer's core) cuts holes inside the views' bounds, for a platform
that rounds views to whole logical pixels (Wayland, patch 0049).

New files: `shaders_native_view.wgsl` (concatenated after each transport), `wgpu_renderer/native_view_hole.rs` (the
hole quads, the pipeline's colour target, the alpha-mode log), `wgpu_renderer/tests/native_view_hole.rs` (table test
`native_view_hole_is_cut_in_paint_order` and `hole_pipeline_exists_only_while_native_views_are_attached`, on the
headless renderer; `native_view_support_names_what_blends_the_window`, pure). `wgpu_renderer.rs` holds only the hooks; `scene.rs` and `window.rs` the attached flag.

Why: the wgpu renderer ignored `PrimitiveBatch::NativeViews`, so no wgpu target could show a native view
(xuckless/parallax#1). The renderer is private to gpui_wgpu, so this can't live in a Parallax crate.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

